/**
 * The single owner of the published direct-adjacency state. It consumes the {@link AdjacencyCommitLog} in revision
 * order and is the only code that ever publishes an {@link AdjacencyState}. Building, catching up, compacting, folding,
 * rebuilding, suspending and closing are units of work it performs between log records, so none of them can race a
 * delta apply. Heavy work runs on an {@link AdjacencyWorkerPool}; results come back as units on the applier's inbox,
 * and the applier never joins a worker on its own thread.
 * <p>
 * The loop body is {@link #runOne()}, driven by an {@link ApplierScheduler}: a dedicated thread in production, a
 * stepping scheduler in tests. Every decision in the body is evaluated in a fixed order, and {@link #hasWork()} asks
 * the same questions without acting, so tests can tell when the applier is idle.
 * <p>
 * Threading summary: committers only touch the log; readers only call {@link #acquire} and release acquisitions; worker
 * completion callbacks and the public request methods only enqueue units. All other fields are applier-thread state.
 *
 * @param <B> the base type
 * @param <O> the overlay-set type
 * @param <G> the generation type
 */
